row    R0C11Y03 1900 [20] court is worth more.
label  R0C11Y03 1901 [20] The gold, as agreed.
label  R0C11Y03 1902 [28] Your house's favour instead.
row    R0C11Y26 1900 [43] The Ripper is dead, and the _reward_ gold I
row    R0C11Y26 1900 [39] promised is yours, %pcf. Yet it was the
row    R0C11Y26 1900 [44] women of ___qgiver_'s streets he hunted, and
row    R0C11Y26 1900 [44] no one has ever paid them a copper for their
row    R0C11Y26 1900 [38] fear. Will you take it, or shall they?
label  R0C11Y26 1901 [19] I'll take the gold.
label  R0C11Y26 1902 [27] Give it to those he hunted.
row    R0C11Y16 1900 [42] A holy relic, and a fine one - some temple
row    R0C11Y16 1900 [40] is missing it, and its priests would pay
row    R0C11Y16 1900 [44] well to learn who has it now. Do you hand it
row    R0C11Y16 1900 [22] over, and say nothing?
label  R0C11Y16 1901 [28] Hand it over. I saw nothing.
label  R0C11Y16 1902 [30] Take no gold. Tell the temple.
row    K0C01Y10 1900 [44] _qgiver_ holds out a hand for the Bothdorjii
row    K0C01Y10 1900 [38] Scroll. The truth about the storm that
row    K0C01Y10 1900 [40] wrecked your ship is its price - but the
row    K0C01Y10 1900 [44] scroll wakes the servants of the Princes. Do
row    K0C01Y10 1900 [17] you hand it over?
label  K0C01Y10 1901 [21] Hand over the scroll.
label  K0C01Y10 1902 [21] Burn the scroll here.
row    K0C01Y10 1903 [44] You burned it? You witless %ra! Have you any
row    K0C01Y10 1903 [42] notion what that scroll was worth? Get out
row    K0C01Y10 1903 [42] of my sight. Whoever sent that storm after
row    K0C01Y10 1903 [34] you, you will not hear it from me.
row    A0C01Y01 1900 [43] They're dead - both of them! You have saved
row    A0C01Y01 1900 [42] my life, %pcf. Here is the gold we agreed,
row    A0C01Y01 1900 [43] though the gods know I will need every coin
row    A0C01Y01 1900 [31] if they come again for my land.
label  A0C01Y01 1901 [24] Take the gold we agreed.
label  A0C01Y01 1902 [25] Keep it. Buy stout locks.
